Some things to think about:

My buying experience on EBAY is that if you see a listing from an
individual that says "No returns", you can bank on it being broken.

Quite so! If I'm really interested in something and the ad doesn't
state whether or not it's working, I try to contact the seller and ask. 
I also like LOTS of photos, from every angle and the insides of the 
equipment. Sometimes I can tell if it won't work just from good, 
hi-res photos. eBay can work but to sell successfully, you have to 
put in at least a little bit of effort. At least know what it is you are 
selling and Google it for a little background so you don't sound like 
a fool and call a terminal a computer (that happens a lot!) Products
rarely sell themselves so it's up to you to be a salesperson. I have
about a 96% success ratio selling on eBay. That's because I'm honest,
start the auction at a reasonable price, I respond to inquiries in
a timely manner and I try to anticipate questions and answer them 
in my ad before people have to spend time asking questions by email.
The 4% I didn't sell, I wasn't too keen on selling anyway (my wife
made me put things up for bid;.she didn't specify that I actually had to 
sell it!) The bottom line is make it easy for the customer to buy from you
and don't misrepresent your merchandise. You can do well on eBay.

Tom Lake


