Hmmm, I sometimes buy from ebay and I don't consider myself clueless.
Not all
sellers or buyers are evil.  Hobbiest crowds are small and clickish in
the sense everyone know what
everybody else has, and the same stuff circulates. Not everyone works
in an industry where they know firsthand
of a piece of 'vintage' gear is destined for the dumpster and can
salvage it.  For a while I worked on computer control systems
that were replacements for other systems.  In my time, I've replaced
PDP8s with 11/34s, later those 11/34s with 11/44s, then vaxes, then
microvaxes, and then alphas.  In almost all cases, when the previous
generation machines were decommissioned,
they were wheeled to the dumpster dock.
So, fast fwd 25yrs. I wanted to add core memory to my 8...I found it
on ebay at a *reasonable* price and it worked.  I talked to a few VARs
and *if* they had it, they were charging 'corporate' prices.
Sorry....It has been a very long time since my 8 was critical in an
air traffic control system...It's a hobby.  My motto is when buying
anything, is let the buyer beware.
Yes, there are many ppl on ebay who look at 'old iron' for the scrap
value.  I recently had some dealings with a fellow that had a very
nice dual 11/44 system with 4-5 vt100s, 5-LA36s, 9" tape drives,
Wrightline cabinets will full complement of dec manuals for RSX11 and
the field prints. He stuck it on ebay and made a sale.  When the
seller found out the buyer was scrapping it, he nixed the deal.
Hobbiest didn't want to pay what he thought it was worth...so he is
going to piecemeal it out to clubs, museaums, and ebay.  So, there are
all sorts of ppl out there.
WRT to your situation, have you tried contacting VARs of old gear to
unload your gear?  Dealing with them may be more to your liking.
OTOH, they have their own set of rules...For example, a couple of
organizations I've dealt with would not take 'big' things...so forget
a complete RK05, RA80, etc.  Now, if you provided them boards, small
parts (e.g. drive motors), or small box machines (a bare 11/44 w/o a
corporate cabinet or rack) they would take it. If you gave them a RK05
or RL02, they would tell you  it is not worth their time to
disassemble and test the components, or keep it assembled and test the
entire drive.  Go figure.

When the time comes for you to lighten your computer load, I'd be
interested in knowing about it...I am located in PA and can easily
make the trip.

-John

