The use of the above text places the cursor at line 24
column 132  OR  at a line at the bottom of the screen
which is above line 24  and at a column at the very
right of the screen which is less than column 132.

After that, KED sends the command text "<ESC>[c"
to report where the cursor is actually located on the
screen.  Obviously, if there are more lines available,
the cursor will not be at the bottom.

Another aspect is that KED / KEX allocates a buffer
which can hold the maximum number of characters.

However, modifying only these two aspects is not
sufficient as was discovered when a test was done
using a screen of 50 lines by 80 columns.  In addition,
if KED / KEX is not modified at all, then when 50 lines
are available, the screen can be scrolled to use all
50 lines as the cursor is moved vertically up and all
50 lines appear correctly.  Moving the cursor down
causes the screen to show VERY incorrect information.

Eventually, I will figure out how to correctly modify
the code in KED / KEX in RT-11 to display correctly
when 50 lines are present.

It would seem quite obvious that no one else who
uses RT-11 will be interested since you are the only
person who has responded and you run RSX-11.

>
> In RSX, the number of lines of a terminal is kept by the OS, and 
> programs can read it out, and adapt, if they want to.
> However, EDT, which is the only DEC screen editor, does not handle 
> more than 24 lines, even if your terminal says it have more. EDT 
> however, do handle both 80 and 132 columns. But not (as far as I know) 
> any other widths.

80 columns and 132 columns seem to be the two standard
sizes that the VT100 started with and most other terminals
from DEC retained those two options.  They really are the
most reasonable since 80 columns is standard for 8 1/2"
paper while 132 columns is standard for 15" paper.  With
the LA120, of course, it was possible to change the size
of the characters so that 132 columns were printed on
8 1/2" paper.  MACRO-11 listings are set up to use
132 columns with the wide option.

