In alt.sys.pdp11 Jerome H. Fine <everyone@nospam.com> wrote:
(snip)

> 80 columns and 132 columns seem to be the two standard
> sizes that the VT100 started with and most other terminals
> from DEC retained those two options.  They really are the
> most reasonable since 80 columns is standard for 8 1/2"
> paper while 132 columns is standard for 15" paper.  With
> the LA120, of course, it was possible to change the size
> of the characters so that 132 columns were printed on
> 8 1/2" paper.  MACRO-11 listings are set up to use
> 132 columns with the wide option.

As far back as I know it, 80 columns is the size of an IBM
punched card, and 132 is the width of many IBM line printers.

Many compilers were designed around 80 column input, and that
became popular for video terminals.  VDTs with 72 columns were
not so unusual, but 80 was somewhat better.

For program listings, a compiler could write a file with line
numbers and other data (nesting level, etc.) before each line 
and generate tables of which variables were used, where, etc.,
over 132 columns.  (Usually 133 including the carriage control
character.)

>> What editors are you talking about? EDT is the editor under RSTS/E and 
>> RSX, but I didn't think it was used under RT-11. But most of the 
>> source code for EDT is shared between all DEC OSes. EDT is written in 
>> Bliss, by the way.

I remember EDT from VAX days, but after not so long we switched
to EVE with EDT emulation mode.  

> Then the source code for KED / KEX used in RT-11
> must not be shared.  It is 100 % MACRO-11!!!

-- glen


