Eventually, we will run out of real DEC hardware and will be
forced to switch to emulators.  E11 supports many different
screen sizes for terminals.  The DOS version is limited to a
size that the hardware provides in FULL  SCREEN mode
which is how I usually run.  Under E11, the command:
SHOW  VIDEO
will list the available screen sizes in FULL  SCREEN mode.

Most video cards support a screen size of 24 lines by 80
columns.  A very few also 24 lines by 132 columns.  In
addition, my video card supports 50 lines by 80 columns
and 44 lines by 132 columns.

When I debug a program using SDX.SYS, the other jobs
are frozen, so a larger number of lines per screen will help
quite a bit when I run KEX as a system job to display the
file of the program under development.  I don't print my
listings any more, With a TB disk drive, there is room form
more listings than I can possibly remember to know what
the source details are.

As Johnny also pointed out, I rarely miss an opportunity to
tout E11.  Running at 100 times the speed of a PDP-11/93
doesn't hurt either.

As for the Win32 versions if E11, just:
E11.EXE  /CONSIZE:50x80
when you start E11 to obtain a screen of 50 lines instead
of the 24 line default.  The number of columns can be
changed at execution time (via the normal VT100 ANSI
text commands) to 132 character columns.

So you can actually have any number of lines for your
"terminal" if you are willing to use hardware (that you
probably already have) that runs many times faster than
a PDP-11/93.

Actually, I just heard of another replacement board for
PDP-11/93 board which also runs at about 100 times
the speed of the PDP-11/93.  This board is ALSO a
Qbus, although it uses FOUR slots in a BA23 or a BA123.

In addition, I understand that it might be able to substitute
a PC monitor and keyboard as the "terminal" for the consol
device so that the RS-232 serial speed limitation is removed.
It is made by TLC and sold by resellers to commercial users,
as far as I know, since the price I have heard is almost
certainly beyond what a hobby user will pay.

Jerome Fine


