On Jul 2, 5:44=A0pm, Nomen Nescio <nob...@dizum.com> wrote:
> If you are a hobbyist who likes old stuff and I know from your other
> postings you are, then sometimes it's worth it to go the extra mile to fi=
nd
> stuff a good home even when it's not something you yourself want. Maybe
> somebody will return the favor to you some day.

This is the philosophy I go by.  I actually generally go for
Craigslist first (which is wonderful in the US) because unless it's
something particularly rare, I don't care to make a buck on it.  I had
to offload a pair of nice, gently used Apple monitors for vintage Macs
a while ago, and I posted them up for free on Craigslist and someone
picked them up within a few days.  For something I can ship, eBay can
get a much wider audience (since Craigslist is generally only for
local users), but I try not to charge something exorbitant; I figure
if someone's interested enough, they'll find it and pay a token amount
and cover my shipping.

N.B. on buying old, possibly broken stuff from the estate sale/
liquidator vendors: I got an Apple II+ for $40 in pristine condition
with two drives, a memory expansion and a serial card, labeled "as is,
seems to power up, but doesn't seem to work".  I was prepared to test
every chip on there (the II+ was all 7400-series logic with the
exception of the CPU and ROM), but it turned out that the only problem
was that the voltage was set to 220v.  Sometimes the items aren't
broken so much as the liquidators just want to get rid of it and can't
be bothered to run it through real tests.

Conversely, I got two Defender board sets (arcade game from 1980)
listed "as is" which needed about half the RAM chips replaced (4116
chips are getting hard to find for a reasonable price) and had to hunt
down a badly-behaved RAM decode chip before they'd work, but after
about a day and a half of work, I had two working board sets.  For $80
each, it was a pretty great deal.  I think it all comes down to what
you're willing to put into it.


