> than using emulated PDP-11 memory via the VIRTUAL
> statement in FORTRAN.

What I would do is to create a device driver to control access to such a 
thing.

> In that regard, does RSX-11 FORTRAN support the
> VIRTUAL statement and is the implementation via an
> EMT request or the modification of the PAR registers
> as is done under RT-11?

As I've said any number of times now, no program under RSX can modify 
the PAR/PDR registers themself. It is all under control of the kernel.
Even if you had access to the I/O page under RSX (which you don't), any 
modifications you would do to the PAR/PDR registers will disappear again 
within moments, as RSX loads the values into these registers from it's 
own concept of your memory mappings at every context switch. So, it does 
not even preserve what you might have written there yourself, if you 
were to try that.

So yes, assuming you had a virtual statement in FORTRAN, which mapped to 
memory, it would have do to that mapping through system calls. Now, I 
don't remember totally for sure, but I seem to remember that VIRTUAL 
under FORTRAN (alteast under RSX) only could map to a file, and not 
memory, so that would make this whole question purely theoretical as 
well, but I can check if it can do virtual arrays in memory later.

