I completely understand your refusal to ever allow a user program
under a mapped RSX-11 to have even read access to the IOPAGE
registers, let alone write access.  I was just wondering it there was
any way that it might be possible.

You have confirmed that read / write access is possible via the MTPx
and MFPx instructions, respectively.  What would be "nice" is if those
instructions could be handled on an exception basis and restricted to
users who have the authorization - along with some method to retain
14xxxx in the PSW.  This might be a feature for RSX-11 in the future
when running under an emulator is a standard feature of an ALL  Qbus
hardware implementation (i.e. the Qbus CPU board actually runs under
emulator code for a CPU with a different instruction set which implements
the PDP-11 architecture).

Such a possibility would substantially enhance the capability of RSX-11
with there being a requirement to modify RSX-11 in a manner which
needed only very minor changes.

> Also, I hope you are aware that any RTI or RTT is going to reset the 
> PSW to 17xxxx as well.

Obviously that would be one of the minor (???) changes that
would be required, i.e. retaining 14xxxx in the PSW when
the user program is again placed into execution.

>> The reason that I am suggesting that there might be a valid
>> reason for the PSW to be 14xxxx is specifically under E11
>> when the user is provided access to the actual PC memory
>> via the EMEM.DLL program. I have used it under RT-11
