I guess rt11 could be compared with msdos in terms of functionality,
while rsx might be compared with nt, though some may be horrifed by the
comparison :-). One is essentially a monitor program with io device and
filesystem tacked on, while the other is truly multitasking, where
system resources are shared between many processes and users.

In a multitasking system, system resource and io device protection is
essential to prevent any single process corrupting others, either by
direct access to io device registers, or memory. The whole idea of
having a kernel layer is to provide controlled access methods to system
resources without the need for any knowledge of the underlying hardware.
A form of abstraction that predates oop by decades.

Trying to bypass the layers to access h/w directly is arguably the most
grave sin in software engineering :-)...

Regards,

Chris


