>> user at a time, because the actual device have no way of keeping
>> separate transactions running at the same time.)
> I know that John did a DO(X).SYS for RT-11, Is there one
> available for RSX-11 as well? Please confirm if there is?

There are several. John got one from some guy, whose name escapes me 
right now, which works like a normal privileged program, which grabs the 
device and have a full interface in the program to copy files to/from 
the device to RSX.
I wrote a normal device driver for the thing, so that you can use the 
normal RSX tools to access files on the device. However, the DO device 
is unfortunately so badly designed so that I have to fake it looking 
like a tape device to make it work in a sensible way.

But it works just fine:

.dev do:
DO0:     Public Loaded Type=UNIX
.dir do:"e11"


Directory DO0:
4-JUL-11 23:53

e11                 ;0        0.

Total of 0./0. blocks in 1. file

.

Notice that the DO: device do not report actual file sizes, nor any 
dates, which is sad. Also, I cannot allow several users access the 
device at the same time, since the DO: device is also designed in a way 
that cannot allow that.

>>> If a problem arises in the future and the solution is a couple of
>>> GB of data memory or conversion of a working system which
>>> already has access to the memory, the effort might then be worth
>>> while. At the moment, it looks like the old "solution looking for
>>> a problem" situation and since I am unlikely to ever become even
>>> 10% as capable running RSX-11 as you are (probably will never
>>> run RSX-11 at all), I will make no additional comments on aspects
>>> which fit RT-11 quite well, but which have no place in RSX-11 at
>>> the present time.
>> Running RSX is easy. However, it does put totally different
>> restrictions at your feet than RT-11. But on the other hand, it also
>> gives you totally different capabilities than RT-11. It's a different
>> type of shoe. If you are in an RT-11 mindset, RSX is going to be
>> difficult, yes. But so will any OS, since it's OSes like RT-11 which
>> are the odd one out.
> I have used VMS on a VAX along with RSTS/E and TSX-Plus on
> a PDP-11. So I used to be very aware of multi-job environments.
> However, I rarely use any of them any more except in regard to
> programs which also run under RT-11.
>
> I suspect that it might be possible under E11 to grant exclusive
> access to a device (actually via RSX-11) which can be passed
> to E11 via a password or some other code which then allows
> only that job to execute certain specific (normally illegal) instructions.

If you go through a device driver, the problem is already solved without 
any passwords or other codes sent to E11. Not using a device driver, you 
will not make it work, since E11 does not know when a context switch 
happens in RSX, which means that some other program is running, which 
should not be allowed. So you cannot solve the problem that way.

> If the "solution looking for a problem" never manages to be
> found, then it seems rather doubtful that the use of PC memory
> will ever be resolved under RSX-11.

You are constantly trying to stick the cart in front of the donkey 
instead of behind it, where it should be.

> Since I am probably the only person using it under RT-11 and
> then only for a buffer when I decided to see if it would be a
> useful method to sieve for Prime Numbers, it seems very
> doubtful that any other problem will use this "new" feature.

Well, I can't honestly see any big need for your special solution to 
your special problem. If I were to do something like that in RSX, I'd 
probably stick the data in a file anyway, which means I can already get 
the same amount of data in, without going outside of the current system.
But in general, if you have problems that require large amounts of 
memory to solve, then a PDP-11 is probably the wrong tool. And your 
solution isn't really much help in the general case either, since very 
few large memory problems only need to look at such a small subset as 
one word for a specific instance.
In general, I'd say a disk based solution, where you can work on larger 
entries, while still having very large data sets will probably be 
easier, and just as efficient in the long run.

> However, if you do use the DOS device under RSX-11 to
> move data back and forth between RSX-11 running under
> E11 and a Windows file system, that would be something
> that is very interesting. Please let me know.

I do, except I run e11 under Unix. But the RSX device driver works just 
as fine talking to the device if it's on a Windows machine.

> Also, an RSX-11 flavour of the HD: device is probably useful
> only if speed is an extremely high priority. The HD: device is
> almost certainly faster than MSCP emulation, but if that is not the
> time critical aspect of any implementation, then MSCP can do
> the identical file I/O without the need to introduce a new type
> of hardware into RSX-11. Again, if you do decide to implement
> the HD: device driver in RSX-11, hints on MMU aspects might
> be useful.

Hints on MMU aspects will most likely be totally worthless. RSX always 
runs with the MMU enabled, and there are a whole bunch of routines that 
handles address translations between 16, 18 and 22 bits, absolute 
addresses, virtual addresses in the kernel as well as for the user 
program, and all other aspects needed to be solved, since the problems 
have all been seen so many times already.
Also, if the HD device don't do DMA, it becomes even easier, because 
then I don't even have to deal with some aspects of memory translations, 
but instead can just use the existing functions that store/read the user 
requested buffer word by word for the device driver.

But if you point me to some specification of the HD device, I can tell 
you how much work a device driver would be.

	Johnny


