If I were to design a modern replacement, I would first look at using
current chips rather than old chips.  Otherwise you just trade one
hard-to-find-thing for another hard-to-find-thing.

>The signals needed for DDR are somewhat different, and won't likely
>be easy to generate from older memory systems.

DDR transfers data on both transitions of the clock signal, while
older systems use an entire clock cycle to transfer data.  It seems
that it shouldn't be hard to match the two worlds and hide those
differences on the module.  Put a small FPGA on the module and you
can do pretty much anything you want.

>The supply of non-parity SIMMs for older PC style machines,
>though, should supply enough chips for new implementations.

Yeah, but its harder than using new components because the old
components have to first be removed from existing equipment.
