On 2011-07-26 12.43, Johnny Billquist wrote:
> On 2011-07-26 03.26, m. thompson wrote:
>> Very cool. Is that a super PCL-11 installed above each CPU?
>
> As far as I can tell, above each CPU you see something that looks like
> the control panel for MK-11 memory boxes. However, there appears to be
> way more of them than I'd expect. The other thing that is there is the
> IIST, but since I've never seen those in real life, I don't know if
> perhaps the panels might be related to that as well.
>
> The IIST is the Interprocessor Interrupt and Sanity Timer. That is a
> vital piece of a multiprocessor PDP-11. :-)
>
> What is a PCL-11?

By the way. Thinking a little more, the MK-11 boxes used in an 11/74 are 
not the "normal" MK-11. I can't remember the name of the MK-11 for 
multiprocessor systems right now. But I just remembered/realized that 
each CPU probably have its own control panel for each memory box, which 
is why there are so many control panels.

The MK-11 control panel is used to set the function of the memory box. 
It sets the box base address, the interleave, if ECC is enabled, and 
also if the box is online or not. And it also have some indicators to 
show some status. Since each 11/74 has its own memory bus, connected to 
each memory box, it makes sense that each CPU has its own control panel 
for each box. Although it makes it possible to get a very screwed up 
setup easily. You can thus, have the same memory box appear at different 
addresses for each CPU.

By the way, while talking about this: The 11/74 was designed for high 
availability. One part of that was that you could take a CPU offline 
while the system was running. Reconfigure it a little, and run 
diagnostics on it while the others are running as normal. For this to be 
possible, the ability to set the address of the memory box different for 
each CPU is vital (I hadn't thought about that before, and had wondered 
a lot how they pulled this off). In RSX, you can reconfigure a running 
system to use less memory. But you can only free up memory at the end of 
the memory space. You cannot have holes in the memory. So, if you want 
to run diagnostics on a CPU, you free up the "last" memory box in RSX. 
You then bring the CPU down. You can then load diagnostics in that 
memory box from RSX (there are tools for this). You reconfigure the 
memory box on the CPU that you want to run diagnostics on, so that this 
same memory box is actually at low memory, and then you can run 
diagnostics on it while the rest of the system keeps running.

If you instead wants to do actual hardware work on a memory box, you 
need to free the memory up as well. That can (hopefully) be done by 
first bringing memory use down so that the box is free. You might want 
to reconfigure the hardware around so that this (bad) box is then out of 
memory space, and the other boxes form a contigous memory, and bring 
that in again. The (bad) memory box can be brought offline at this 
point, and then you can power it down, and start fooling around with it, 
as long as you don't mess with the actual memory bus cables.

The 11/74 with RSX is a way cool system, and in some ways way more 
advanced and capable than any modern multiprocessor systems I know of today.

	Johnny



