As far as I can tell, PCL-11 can be regarded as an interface to a small 
network of max 16 nodes. You can send data to other nodes, and that's 
about it. Pretty much the same deal as ethernet.

Yes, the PCL-11 predates ethernet, which probably is much of the reason 
it was created. The DMA thing is no different than ethernet, though. Or 
what do you mean?

The PCL-11 sounds like a cool piece of equipment, and I can definitely 
see that it had a place back in the day. However, in todays view, 
ethernet solves the same problem better (higher speed, more nodes 
possible, longer distances).

So, no defence needed. I like the stuff, even if I probably wouldn't use 
it today. I was just making a comment about it's usability today.

As in relation to the 11/74 pictures, there are no PCL-11 in use there. 
No need, as I said. But that does not in any way reflect on the PCL-11 
in any way. It's just not designed for, or useful in such a system.

	Johnny


