urbancamo <mark@wickensonline.co.uk> wrote:
> Thought I was going mad, but I'm not.
> According to the AS1000A Service Guide Bank 0 must contain a memory
> option (the lowest bank in the diagram). However, I've found that the
> machine won't boot without Bank 3 being populated. Note also that it
> specifies five SIMMs  as required. However, when I got the machine it
> had Banks 3-1 populated with no SIMMs in the ECC bank.

There seems to be quiet some confusion here. The 4/XXX systems using an EV4
or 21064 processor need the additional 5th ECC SIMM. The 5/XXX don't. The
reason is that ECC is done more efficiently (but less accurately) on the
EV5 machines. I forgot the exact details, but I think it was that the EV4
could detect a memory error in every 128bits, while the EV5 could only
detect an error in every 256bits. Please don't trust me on the exact number
of bits, but it was something like that.

The EV4 systems can be upgraded to EV5. The process involves upgrading the
firmware and then exchanging the CPU card. When going from EV4 to EV5 one
can simply remove the ECC SIMMs as they are no longer used. Later mainboard
revisions had no slots for ECC SIMMs anymore and therefore cannot use an
EV4 processor card.

In my experience, the AS1000 uses regular FPM SIMMs with real 36bit parity.
That's pretty standard stuff when you are dealing with old machines. Some
years ago, I filled empty slots with leftover 4MB SIMMs. There is no need
to focus on DEC branded memory modules.

HTH
Dennis

