 >Hans Vlems wrote:

> I ran RT-11 V4 on an 11/40 because it had KED and the 11/40 was
>equiped with a VT100 clone (a C.Itoh 101 IIRC).
>Before that I ran RT-11 V3.? on an LA36. I very much preferred the
>VT100 clone. KED did need more (core) memory.
>RT-11 V5.03 still had KED
>
I just checked a few of the dozen or so binary releases
of RT-11 up to V05.03 of RT-11.  As far as I can see:

Version      Date              Editors

V2.0c        Nov-1975     EDIT
V3.0          Aug-1977     EDIT  /  TECO
V3.0b        Mar-1979     EDIT  /  TECO
V04.00      Feb-1980      EDIT  /  TECO  /  KED
V05.00      Mar-1983     EDIT  /  TECO  /  KED  /  KEX
V05.01      FEB-1984     EDIT  /  KED  /  KEX

As far as I know, EDIT  /  KED  /  KEX  remain on all
releases up to V05.07 of RT-11 with TECO "magically"
also reappearing on V05.07, a file that is identical to the
file released in Mar-1983 with V05.00 of RT-11.

Since almost all of the PDP-11 systems that I run (these days)
have the full 4 MB of memory available, memory is rarely a
problem EXCEPT when I run a number of system jobs of
KEX (usually at least 4 with n=1 to 4):

SRUN  KEX.SAV/LEVEL:n/TERMINAL:n/NAME:Kn

where each system job requires 801. words of low memory
in addition to around 52 KB of extended memory.  While
reducing the low memory for each system job to 545. words
takes a bit of work (mostly to modify the XHANDL overlay
handler - and also increases the required extended memory
mostly to re-locate the stack into extended memory), my
real goal was to have KED / KEX support more than 24
lines.  Even fewer low memory words may be possible -
a work in progress - the use of auxiliary files may not be
possible if the low memory for a system job is reduced
below 545. words.  However, since when I run KEX as
a system job, it is usually just to INSPECT the file, that
may be a good compromise if the number of low memory
words can be reduced to 449. words for each KEX job.

As Johnny has mentioned, there no longer seems to be many
individuals running RT-11 who are interested in the software.
Most like to work with the old PDP-11 hardware, so the
details of how an editor runs and how much memory it uses
are extremely low on an priority list.

Is there anyone else who would like a copy of any RT-11
bug fixes and enhancements?  In particular, on the subject
of KED / KEX, my goal is to increase the number of lines
to at least 50 (probably around 65 so that a full page of a
MACRO-11 listing can be displayed at a time).

An even more important objective would be to share the
concepts of any enhancements to RT-11 with another
individual who is also interested in software like myself.
Does anyone qualify?  Is anyone else interested?  Would
anyone be interested in composing a list of bug fixes and
enhancements for RT-11?  There was a "Wish  List" for
RT-11 about 20 years ago, maybe longer that I should
be able to find on one of the DECUS tapes.  Would
anyone be interested?

Jerome Fine

